(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-2-circulatory-physiology-1-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Circulatory Physiology I — Physiology Exam 2 — Set 2","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"What is the structure of the capillary wall, and what are the two routes for solutes/water to cross it?","choices":["A multilayered stratified epithelium several cells thick; solutes cross only through active, energy-dependent transport pumps","Capillary walls are completely impermeable to all substances, relying entirely on the lymphatic system for tissue exchange","A unicellular layer of endothelial cells surrounded by a basement membrane; solutes/water cross via intercellular clefts or plasmalemmal vesicles","A thick layer of smooth muscle with no endothelial lining at all, similar in structure to a small arteriole"],"answer":2,"exp":"The capillary wall is composed of a unicellular layer of endothelial cells surrounded by a basement membrane (capillary diameter 4-9 microns); solutes and water cross via intercellular clefts (spaces between cells) or via plasmalemmal vesicles. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How do lipid-soluble substances (e.g., CO2, O2) cross the capillary wall, compared to lipid-insoluble substances (e.g., water, Na+, glucose)?","choices":["Both cross exclusively via intercellular clefts, since the capillary cell membrane itself is entirely impermeable","Lipid-insoluble substances diffuse directly through the capillary membrane, while lipid-soluble substances require plasmalemmal vesicle transport","Lipid-soluble substances diffuse directly through the capillary cell membrane; lipid-insoluble substances cross via intercellular clefts","Lipid-soluble substances cannot cross the capillary wall at all, requiring active transport via specific membrane carrier proteins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lipid-soluble substances like CO2 and O2 diffuse directly through the capillary endothelial cell membrane, while lipid-insoluble substances like water, Na+, Cl-, and glucose cross via intercellular clefts (spaces between adjacent endothelial cells). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the approximate width of the intercellular slit pores in capillary walls?","choices":["600-700 nanometers","6-7 micrometers","6-7 nanometers","6-7 millimeters"],"answer":2,"exp":"The width of capillary intercellular slit pores is about 6-7 nanometers, and capillary permeability to different substances varies according to their molecular diameter relative to this pore size. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Based on the relative permeability table, how does albumin's capillary permeability compare to water's?","choices":["Molecular weight has no effect on capillary permeability, which instead depends only on a molecule's electrical charge","Albumin is far less permeable than water (about 0.0001 vs 1.00), due to its much larger molecular weight","Albumin is more permeable than water (about 1.5 vs 1.00), despite its considerably larger molecular weight","Albumin and water have nearly identical permeability, since intercellular clefts admit molecules of any size equally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Albumin (molecular weight ~69,000) has a relative permeability of about 0.0001 compared to water's permeability of 1.00, illustrating how capillary permeability decreases dramatically as molecular size increases. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What are the two major solid structures found in the interstitium, and in what form is most interstitial fluid found?","choices":["Muscle fibers and nerve fibers; most fluid exists as freely mobile liquid rather than a bound gel","Collagen fibers and proteoglycan filaments; most fluid exists as a gel (proteoglycan-fluid mixture) rather than free fluid","Only free-flowing fluid with no solid structures, allowing rapid unrestricted diffusion of all interstitial proteins","Elastin fibers and reticular fibers only; virtually all interstitial fluid exists in a completely free-flowing state"],"answer":1,"exp":"The interstitium contains collagen fibers and proteoglycan filaments (coiled hyaluronic acid molecules) as its major solid structures; almost all interstitial fluid exists as a gel (fluid-proteoglycan mixture), with very little free fluid under normal conditions. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What direction does capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc) tend to move fluid?","choices":["It only affects albumin movement, not water or small solutes","Inward, from the interstitium back into the capillary","Outward, through the capillary membrane into the interstitium","It has no measurable effect on interstitial fluid volume"],"answer":2,"exp":"Capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc) tends to force fluid outward through the capillary membrane, into the interstitial space — one of the four Starling forces governing capillary fluid exchange. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What direction does plasma colloid osmotic pressure move fluid, and what causes changes in it?","choices":["It promotes filtration outward, driving fluid into the interstitium; changes are caused mainly by variations in red blood cell concentration","It has no measurable effect on capillary fluid movement, since only hydrostatic pressures govern net filtration and reabsorption","It opposes filtration, causing osmosis of water inward; changes are caused by conditions like cirrhosis, nephrosis, or dilutional states affecting plasma protein concentration","It only affects lymphatic flow, not capillary exchange, and it changes only with alterations in lymphatic pump activity or valve competence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Plasma colloid osmotic pressure opposes filtration, causing osmosis of water inward through the capillary membrane; conditions like cirrhosis, nephrosis, or dilutional states (which lower plasma protein concentration) reduce this pressure and promote edema. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What percentage of total plasma colloid osmotic pressure comes from albumin versus globulins?","choices":["100% albumin, 0% globulins","25% albumin, 75% globulins","75% albumin, 25% globulins","50% albumin, 50% globulins"],"answer":2,"exp":"About 75% of total plasma colloid osmotic pressure results from albumin, and about 25% is due to globulins (with a small additional contribution from fibrinogen). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is the equation for net filtration pressure (NP) across a capillary, incorporating all four Starling forces?","choices":["NP = Pc − πp + Pif − πif","NP = Pc + πp + Pif + πif","NP is independent of any of these forces","NP = Pc − πp − Pif + πif"],"answer":3,"exp":"Net filtration pressure is NP = Pc − πp − Pif + πif, where Pc is capillary hydrostatic pressure (outward), πp is plasma colloid osmotic pressure (inward), Pif is interstitial fluid pressure (opposes filtration if positive), and πif is interstitial colloid osmotic pressure (outward, promoting filtration). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What is normal interstitial fluid pressure in most tissues, and what is it in encapsulated organs?","choices":["Normally negative (about -3 mm Hg) in most tissues, but positive (+5 to +10 mm Hg) in encapsulated organs, with the negative pressure attributed to lymphatic pumping","Always zero regardless of tissue type, since interstitial fluid pressure is fully equalized by continuous lymphatic drainage","Normally positive (+10 mm Hg) in most tissues, but negative (-5 mm Hg) in encapsulated organs, attributed to their surrounding capsule","Always equal to capillary hydrostatic pressure, since the two compartments are freely connected via intercellular clefts"],"answer":0,"exp":"Interstitial fluid pressure in most tissues is normally slightly negative (about -3 mm Hg), caused by pumping of the lymphatic system, while encapsulated organs have positive interstitial pressures (+5 to +10 mm Hg) due to their surrounding capsule. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"At the arteriolar end of a typical capillary, is the net force outward (filtration) or inward (absorption), and what is its approximate magnitude?","choices":["Net outward force of about 13 mm Hg (filtration)","Net inward force of about 7 mm Hg (absorption)","No net force — inward and outward forces cancel exactly","Net outward force of about 28 mm Hg"],"answer":0,"exp":"At the arteriolar end of the capillary, total outward force (about 41 mm Hg, mainly capillary pressure) exceeds total inward force (about 28 mm Hg, plasma colloid osmotic pressure), yielding a net outward filtration force of about 13 mm Hg. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"At the venous end of a typical capillary, is the net force outward or inward, and what is its approximate magnitude?","choices":["Net outward force of about 13 mm Hg (filtration), since capillary pressure remains high along the venous end","No net force at the venous end, since hydrostatic and osmotic pressures are exactly equal at that point","Net outward force of about 5 mm Hg, since capillary pressure remains slightly elevated at the venous end","Net inward force of about 7 mm Hg (reabsorption), since capillary pressure has fallen along the capillary length"],"answer":3,"exp":"At the venous end of the capillary, capillary pressure has fallen (to about 10 mm Hg) while plasma colloid osmotic pressure remains about 28 mm Hg, producing a net inward force of about 7 mm Hg that reabsorbs fluid back into the capillary. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What is the approximate net filtration rate for the entire body, given a mean net filtration pressure of about 0.3 mm Hg?","choices":["20 L/min","200 mL/min","0 mL/min — there is no net filtration","2 mL/min"],"answer":3,"exp":"The mean net filtration pressure across capillaries throughout the body is about 0.3 mm Hg, producing a net filtration rate of approximately 2 mL/min for the entire body — this small excess is returned to the circulation via the lymphatics. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What percentage of filtered capillary fluid is reabsorbed at the venous end, and what happens to the remainder?","choices":["None of the filtered fluid is ever reabsorbed, with the entire filtered volume returning via the lymphatics","50% is reabsorbed at the venous end; the remaining 50% is returned via the lymphatic system","90% is reabsorbed at the venous end; the remaining 10% is returned to circulation via the lymphatics","100% is reabsorbed at the venous end, with none requiring lymphatic drainage under normal conditions"],"answer":2,"exp":"About 90% of filtered fluid is reabsorbed at the venous end of the capillary; the remaining 10% is returned to the circulation via the lymphatic system, preventing net fluid accumulation (edema) under normal conditions. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What are the major functions of the lymphatic system described in this lecture?","choices":["An accessory route for fluid/protein return to blood (preventing edema), a major route for GI nutrient absorption, and a role in immune function","Regulating heart rate directly through parasympathetic fibers that run alongside the major lymphatic trunks","Only removing metabolic waste products, playing no role whatsoever in immunity, edema prevention, or nutrient absorption","Producing red blood cells exclusively within specialized lymphoid tissue found throughout the mesentery"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lymphatic system serves as an accessory route for fluid and protein to flow from interstitial spaces back to the blood (important in preventing edema), is a major route for absorption of nutrients from the GI tract, and plays an important role in the immune system. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What structural feature of lymphatic capillaries allows easy entry of high-molecular-weight substances and proteins while preventing backflow?","choices":["A continuous basement membrane with no gaps, restricting entry to only very small lipid-soluble molecules","Overlapping endothelial cells that form valves preventing backflow while allowing easy entry of large molecules/proteins","Lymphatic capillaries have no valve-like structures at all, relying entirely on external muscle compression for flow","Tight junctions with no overlap between cells, preventing entry of any protein larger than albumin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Overlap between endothelial cells in lymphatic capillaries forms flap-like valves that prevent backflow of lymph while allowing easy entry of high-molecular-weight substances and proteins that cannot easily enter blood capillaries. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What two major factors determine the rate of lymph flow?","choices":["Lymph flow is constant and unregulated, remaining completely independent of both interstitial pressure and any muscular pumping activity","Only blood pressure in the aorta, which is transmitted passively downstream through the entire lymphatic vessel network","Only the activity of the lymphatic pump itself, since interstitial fluid pressure has no measurable influence on flow rate","Interstitial fluid (IF) pressure and the activity of the lymphatic pump (smooth muscle contraction, external compression, arterial pulsation, and valves)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Lymph flow is a function of interstitial fluid (IF) pressure and the activity of the lymphatic pump — driven by smooth muscle contraction in lymph vessel walls, external compression (e.g., skeletal muscle), pulsation of surrounding arteries, and one-way lymphatic valves. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What determines how much blood flow a given tissue receives under local control?","choices":["Each tissue controls its own blood flow in proportion to its needs (oxygen delivery, nutrient delivery, waste removal), closely related to metabolic rate","Blood flow is fixed and unrelated to tissue activity, determined entirely by vessel anatomy that was established during fetal development","Blood flow is determined solely by total circulating blood volume, entirely independent of local tissue metabolic demand or activity level","Only the autonomic nervous system determines local tissue blood flow, with no contribution from local metabolic factors whatsoever"],"answer":0,"exp":"Each tissue controls its own blood flow in proportion to its needs — delivery of oxygen and nutrients, removal of CO2 and other metabolites, and transport of hormones — with flow closely related to the tissue's metabolic rate. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"According to the vasodilator theory of local blood flow control, which substances act as vasodilators released by metabolically active tissue?","choices":["Norepinephrine, epinephrine, angiotensin II, and vasopressin acting on smooth muscle","Adenosine, CO2, lactic acid, ADP compounds, histamine, K+ ions, and H+ ions","Albumin, globulin, fibrinogen, and other large circulating plasma proteins","Oxygen, glucose, and free fatty acids delivered directly to arteriolar smooth muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vasodilator theory proposes that metabolically active tissue releases vasodilator substances — adenosine, CO2, lactic acid, ADP compounds, histamine, K+ ions, and H+ ions — that increase local blood flow to meet metabolic demand. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What does the oxygen demand theory propose about the relationship between oxygen availability and local blood flow?","choices":["Oxygen availability has no effect on local blood flow, which instead responds only to changes in local CO2 concentration","Only CO2, not oxygen, affects local blood flow, providing the primary acute feedback signal for tissue perfusion","Reduced oxygen availability decreases blood flow further, worsening tissue hypoxia through a positive feedback loop","Reduced oxygen availability increases blood flow, providing acute local feedback control matching flow to oxygen need"],"answer":3,"exp":"The oxygen demand theory proposes that reduced oxygen availability to a tissue increases local blood flow, providing acute feedback control that helps match blood flow delivery to the tissue's oxygen needs. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What is autoregulation of blood flow?","choices":["The ability of a tissue to maintain blood flow relatively constant over a wide range of arterial pressures","A phenomenon that only occurs in the heart and kidneys, absent from skeletal muscle and skin circulation","A mechanism requiring direct cortical control, mediated entirely through descending sympathetic pathways","The inability of any tissue to control its own blood flow independent of central nervous system regulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Autoregulation is the ability of a tissue to maintain blood flow relatively constant over a wide range of arterial pressures, explained by both metabolic theory (pressure drop → reduced O2/nutrient delivery → vasodilator release) and myogenic theory (arterioles intrinsically dilate in response to decreased wall tension). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"According to Laplace's law, how is the wall tension of a hollow structure (like a blood vessel) related to internal pressure, radius, and wall thickness?","choices":["Wall tension is unrelated to any of these variables","Wall tension only depends on wall thickness","Wall tension is inversely related to internal pressure and radius, and directly related to wall thickness","Wall tension is directly related to internal pressure and radius, and inversely related to wall thickness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Laplace's law states that the wall tension of a hollow structure (vessel, ventricle, alveolus) is directly related to internal pressure and radius, and inversely related to wall thickness — thicker walls reduce wall tension for a given pressure and radius. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"How does long-term regulation of blood flow occur, compared to acute mechanisms?","choices":["Long-term regulation does not really exist as a distinct process; only acute vasodilator and myogenic mechanisms actually operate to control tissue blood flow","By changing heart rate exclusively, with absolutely no accompanying change in the size or number of vessels supplying a given tissue","By instantaneous changes in vessel diameter only, occurring within seconds and requiring no structural vascular remodeling whatsoever over time","By changing the degree of vascularity of tissues (size and number of vessels), with oxygen as an important stimulus; long-term mechanisms are more effective than acute ones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Long-term regulation of blood flow occurs by changing the degree of vascularity of tissues (altering the size and number of vessels present), with oxygen serving as an important stimulus for this vascular remodeling; long-term mechanisms are more effective than acute regulatory mechanisms. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"What is angiogenesis, and what triggers it?","choices":["A process exclusive to the developing fetus, which ceases entirely and permanently once vascular development is complete at birth","A process with no known trigger, occurring randomly and unpredictably throughout all tissues regardless of metabolic demand","The destruction of existing blood vessels, triggered specifically by prolonged exposure to high oxygen tension within the tissue","The growth of new blood vessels, occurring in response to angiogenic factors released from ischemic tissue, rapidly growing tissue, or tissue with high metabolic rates"],"answer":3,"exp":"Angiogenesis is the growth of new blood vessels, occurring in response to angiogenic factors (such as VEGF, FGF, and angiogenin) released from ischemic tissue, rapidly growing tissue, or tissue with high metabolic rates. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which of the following is a humoral (bloodborne) vasoconstrictor agent?","choices":["Histamine","Angiotensin II","Nitric oxide","Bradykinin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Angiotensin II is a potent humoral vasoconstrictor, along with norepinephrine, epinephrine, vasopressin, and endothelin; bradykinin, serotonin, histamine, prostaglandins, and nitric oxide are humoral vasodilators. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"Can higher brain centers, such as the hypothalamus, influence the vasomotor center (VMC)?","choices":["Yes — many higher centers of the brain, such as the hypothalamus, can exert powerful excitatory or inhibitory effects on the VMC","Only the cerebellum can influence the VMC, exerting exclusively inhibitory control over its baseline firing rate","Higher brain centers can only inhibit, never excite, the VMC, regardless of the emotional or physiological state involved","No, the VMC operates completely independently of higher brain centers, receiving input only from peripheral baroreceptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Many higher centers of the brain, including the hypothalamus, can exert powerful excitatory or inhibitory effects on the vasomotor center, integrating broader physiological and emotional states into cardiovascular control. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"What is the approximate normal interstitial colloid osmotic pressure, and what causes it?","choices":["About 25 mm Hg, driven by an unusually high interstitial albumin concentration","About 28 mm Hg, identical to plasma colloid osmotic pressure due to unrestricted protein exchange","0 mm Hg, since capillary walls are entirely impermeable to any plasma protein","About 8 mm Hg, caused by an interstitial protein concentration of approximately 3 g/dL"],"answer":3,"exp":"Interstitial colloid osmotic pressure is normally about 8 mm Hg, resulting from an interstitial protein concentration of approximately 3 g/dL — lower than plasma's protein concentration but still exerting a meaningful osmotic pull that promotes filtration. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What two factors make up the filtration coefficient (Kf) used to calculate filtration rate?","choices":["Blood viscosity and the length of the capillary segment","Plasma protein concentration and net interstitial fluid pressure","Cardiac output and mean systemic arterial pressure","Surface area and the hydraulic conductivity of the membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"The filtration coefficient (Kf) is the product of the capillary surface area and the hydraulic conductivity of the membrane; filtration rate = net filtration pressure (NFP) multiplied by Kf. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How does an increase in hematocrit affect blood viscosity and vascular resistance?","choices":["Increased hematocrit decreases blood viscosity slightly, lowering overall vascular resistance","Increased hematocrit only affects oxygen-carrying capacity, not vessel resistance at all","Increased hematocrit increases blood viscosity, which increases vascular resistance","Hematocrit has no relationship to blood viscosity, which depends only on plasma protein levels"],"answer":2,"exp":"An increase in hematocrit increases blood viscosity, which in turn increases vascular resistance (consistent with Poiseuille's law, where flow is inversely proportional to viscosity). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Approximately how many capillaries are estimated to exist in the body, and what total surface area do they provide for exchange?","choices":["About 100 million capillaries, with a surface area of roughly 50 square meters","Capillary number and surface area vary too much to ever be reasonably estimated","Over 10 billion capillaries, with a surface area of 500-700 square meters","About 1 billion capillaries, with a surface area of 100 square meters"],"answer":2,"exp":"The microcirculation contains over 10 billion capillaries, providing a total surface area of 500-700 square meters for the transport of nutrients and removal of waste products via solute and fluid exchange. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 50"}]};
